    {
      "line": 80,
      "upstream": "cd | pwd - cd doesn't affect next command in pipeline",
      "disposition": "case",
      "cases": [
        {
          "id": "pipeline_stack/cd-pwd-cd-doesn-t-affect-next-command-in-pipeline",
          "template": ["cd / | pwd"],
          "expect": {
            "stdout": "{{TEMP_NATIVE}}\n",
            "exitCode": 0
          },
          "note": "Upstream uses a stdout callback that returns a boolean (s.includes(\"$TEMP_DIR\")) which TestBuilder ignores, so upstream effectively checks nothing; encoded real Bun 1.4.2 output: cd in a pipeline runs in its own copy of the shell state, so pwd prints the unchanged temp dir. On Windows the shell cwd, as printed by `pwd` and `$PWD`, uses backslashes"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "line": 85,
      "upstream": "cd | cd | pwd - multiple cd's don't affect",
      "disposition": "case",
      "cases": [
        {
          "id": "pipeline_stack/cd-cd-pwd-multiple-cd-s-don-t-affect",
          "template": ["mkdir foo; mkdir foo/bar; cd foo | cd foo/bar | pwd"],
          "expect": {
            "stdout": "{{TEMP_NATIVE}}\n",
            "exitCode": 0
          },
          "note": "Upstream uses a stdout callback that returns a boolean (s.includes(\"$TEMP_DIR\")) which TestBuilder ignores, so upstream effectively checks nothing; encoded real Bun 1.4.2 output: cd in a pipeline runs in its own copy of the shell state, so pwd prints the unchanged temp dir. On Windows the shell cwd, as printed by `pwd` and `$PWD`, uses backslashes"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "line": 93,
      "upstream": "pwd | cd | pwd - cd in middle doesn't affect",
      "disposition": "case",
      "cases": [
        {
          "id": "pipeline_stack/pwd-cd-pwd-cd-in-middle-doesn-t-affect",
          "template": ["pwd | cd / | pwd"],
          "expect": {
            "stdout": "{{TEMP_NATIVE}}\n",
            "exitCode": 0
          },
          "note": "Upstream uses a stdout callback that returns a boolean (s.includes(\"$TEMP_DIR\")) which TestBuilder ignores, so upstream effectively checks nothing; encoded real Bun 1.4.2 output: cd in a pipeline runs in its own copy of the shell state, so pwd prints the unchanged temp dir. Upstream's callback expected 2 lines, but the first pwd's output is piped into cd (which ignores stdin), so only the last pwd's line is printed. On Windows the shell cwd, as printed by `pwd` and `$PWD`, uses backslashes"
        }
      ]
    },
